// entities.def -- entity definition DSL.
//
// Parsed by src/tools/def_gen.cpp, which emits generated/entities_generated.*
//
// `mesh_asset` and `texture_asset` are asset CLASSES, and they are declared
// nowhere: asset_pack walks resources/ and writes generated/assets.manifest,
// which def_gen reads through --asset-manifest. There is nothing to import and
// nothing to edit -- adding a mesh means dropping a .obj or .mesh into a
// resource directory. An asset-typed field here is spelled assets::mesh_asset
// in the generated structs, because the asset artifacts land in namespace
// `assets` beside the asset system that resolves the ids.
//
// Transcribed 2026-07-26 from the macro declarations in this folder; flags
// audited field by field 2026-07-27 (P4). See entity_def.md at the repo root
// for the design, todo.md for the phase order.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// DELIBERATELY ABSENT: the geometry types
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// AABB_Entity, Wedge_Entity, Static_Mesh_Entity and Displacement_Entity are
// NOT here. Per entity_def.md they leave the entity system entirely and become
// plain map-owned C++ value types with handwritten map I/O -- they are never
// networked, so they were paying the blittable/fixed-size constraints for
// nothing. Displacement's [3267]f32 height array is the only reason the DSL
// would ever need fixed-capacity arrays; with geometry gone, it doesn't.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT THE THREE FLAGS MEAN  (P4, 2026-07-27 -- these are now real)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// In the macro system only @Editable was ever enforced, so @Networked and
// @Saveable could sit on a field and mean nothing. The generator makes all
// three load-bearing. Every field below has been decided, not inherited:
//
//   @Networked  The server replicates this field to clients in snapshots.
//               Earn it: the client must READ the field AND have no other way
//               to get it. A map-placed entity's static config is already on
//               the client -- it loaded the same map -- so replicating it is
//               bandwidth spent to tell the client what it already knows.
//
//   @Editable   The editor inspector shows and edits it. The inspector only
//               ever walks map entities, so this is unreachable on a
//               @runtime_only type -- and the generator now rejects it there.
//
//   @Saveable   Written to and read from the .source map file. Same
//               reachability rule as @Editable, same generator check.
//
// Two structural rules the generator enforces, both added in P4:
//   * A component-typed field carries NO flags. The flags on the component's
//     own fields are what every consumer reads; a flag at the use site would
//     be silently ignored. (`volume: Box_Volume @Editable` used to do exactly
//     that, sitting next to four unflagged component fields that meant the
//     same thing.)
//   * @Editable / @Saveable on a @runtime_only entity is an error, not a
//     no-op. The base's fields are exempt by construction: `Entity` is not
//     itself @runtime_only, and its flags describe the map-placed types.
//
// A component-typed field carries no flags, but it CAN carry a default, written
// as a literal naming the fields it differs on:
//
//     render: Render = { mesh = .Leet_Full }
//
// A field the literal does not name keeps the component's own default, so the
// component stays the one place a neutral value is written and a use site says
// only what is special about it. Literals nest (`{ material = { color = ... } }`)
// and their order does not matter -- the generator sorts them.
//
// This exists so a per-type value has ONE home. Every one of these used to be a
// hand-written assignment in a spawn function, and they had already drifted:
// bot-fired rockets lived 5 seconds while player-fired ones lived 20, and a
// trigger volume was born {1,1,1} everywhere except the editor's placement tool,
// which said {64,64,64}.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ORIENTATION UNITS  (decided 2026-07-27; documented here, not yet enforced)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// CANONICAL: `orientation` is Euler XYZ in DEGREES. Not radians, not a
// quaternion.
//
// That is a description of what the code already does, arrived at by reading
// it rather than by preference:
//   * renderer.cpp:2864 multiplies draw_mesh's rotation by DEG2RAD, so every
//     draw site is feeding it degrees.
//   * physics_body_system.cpp:122 writes `quat_to_euler_degrees(jolt_rotation)`
//     back into orientation, so the physics bridge agrees.
// Radians appear nowhere in stored orientation data. So the ambiguity was in
// the DOCUMENTATION, not in two halves of the code disagreeing.
//
// Why not quaternions, given they are the correct storage for a rotation:
// because switching is a real migration (every draw site, the editor gizmo,
// the Jolt bridge, map I/O), and because the thing that makes quaternions
// worth it -- slerping between snapshots -- is not built yet. When snapshot
// interpolation lands (todo.md, physics bodies), revisit: that is the point at
// which euler starts costing correctness rather than just elegance.
//
// KNOWN INCONSISTENCY, left alone deliberately: editor_gizmo.cpp:385-399 packs
// euler xyz into a vec4 with w=0 in one branch and writes an identity
// QUATERNION {0,0,0,1} in another. Those two cannot both be right. It is a
// gizmo-local bug, not an ambiguity about what `orientation` means, so it is
// tracked under Correctness in todo.md rather than resolved here.
//
// NOT the same thing as heading. A Player's facing direction is
// view_angle_yaw / view_angle_pitch, and its motion is `velocity`, a vector.
// `orientation` on a Player is written once at spawn (server_impl.cpp:455) and
// never updated -- it seeds the view angles and is then vestigial. That is why
// there is no relation to enforce between orientation and velocity: they
// answer different questions, and for the entity where both exist, only one is
// live.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Base
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Fully_Serializable :: [@Networked, @Editable, @Saveable]

Entity :: base {
    // The receiver keys the entity by this, so it must ride the wire. It is
    // assigned by the session, never by an author: the map file carries its
    // own uid per entry, so this is neither editable nor saved.
    entity_id:   u32                    @Networked

    // Networked because the types that MOVE (Player, Rocket, Physics_Body) all
    // inherit it. Editable and saved because the types that DON'T move are all
    // placed by hand.
    position:    v3                     @Fully_Serializable
    orientation: v3                     @Fully_Serializable
}



// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Enums -- every one of these was a string or a bare int in the macro system
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Light_Type :: enum { Point, Spot, Directional }

Spawn_Type :: enum { Human, Bot }
Team_Allegiance :: enum {Red, Blu, Free_For_All}

Weapon :: enum {Knife, Scout, Rocket_Launcher}
Weapon_Kind :: enum { Melee, Hitscan, Projectile, Sniper }

Shader_Type :: enum { Lit, Unlit }
// Physics_Body_Entity's shape, and the only user. Capsule was here for the old
// static player hitbox; nothing writes one now and spawn_physics_body has never
// been able to build one, so a value that can only ever produce a log_error is
// not a value worth having.
Shape_Kind :: enum { Sphere, Box }



Trigger_Action :: enum { Kill, Set_Health, Print_Message, Warp_To_Spawn }
Fire_Mode :: enum { On_Enter, Every_Tick }

// The five authored aim poses (animation_def.md §5). No entity field has this
// type and no id rides the wire -- it is here for the generated `_COUNT` and
// the `enum_traits` specialization, because it sizes an Enum_Array in three
// places and a hand-written enum can only get a count from a `Count` sentinel
// that then needs a dead `case` in every switch.
//
// The ORDER is the pose space's, not alphabetical: Forward is the centre of the
// plus that compute_aim_blend interpolates over, and the pairs are opposites.
Aim_Pose :: enum { Forward, Upward, Downward, Left, Right }



// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Components
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// A component's flags are shared by every entity that embeds it -- there is no
// per-use masking, by decision (see todo.md). So a component's flags are the
// union of what its USERS need, and the audit below reasons about the users.

// was: shared::box_volume_t
//
// NOT @Networked. Its only user is Trigger_Volume_Entity, which is invisible
// and entirely server-side: no client reads a trigger's bounds, and if one
// ever did it would be reading the map it already loaded. Previously
// @Fully_Serializable, which put a position and half-extents on the wire for
// every trigger in the level, for nobody.
Box_Volume :: component {
    position: v3 = {0, 0, 0}            @Editable @Saveable
    half_extents: v3 = {1, 1, 1}        @Editable @Saveable
}

// was: network::material_t
// Reached only through Render, so it inherits Render's reasoning below.
Material :: component {
    shader_type: Shader_Type = .Lit     @Fully_Serializable
    color:       v3 = {1, 1, 1}         @Fully_Serializable
    roughness:   f32 = 0.5              @Fully_Serializable
}

Render :: component {
    mesh:         mesh_asset = .Missing @Fully_Serializable
    visible:      bool = true           @Fully_Serializable
    is_wireframe: bool = false          @Fully_Serializable
    offset:       v3 = {0, 0, 0}        @Fully_Serializable
    scale:        v3 = {1, 1, 1}        @Fully_Serializable
    rotation:     v3 = {0, 0, 0}        @Fully_Serializable
    material:     Material
}

// What a player CARRIES, and the one gate that belongs to switching rather than
// to any weapon. Per-weapon state -- ammo, and when that weapon may next fire --
// lives on the Weapon_Entity itself, so this holds handles and nothing else.
//
// The inventory is STORED, not derived. Scanning every Weapon_Entity for one
// whose owner is this player would leave the invariant with no home and make
// "what am I carrying" a linear walk over the world.
Inventory :: component {
    // uid of the Weapon_Entity for each weapon type; null_entity_uid for one
    // not carried. Keyed by the weapon rather than a flat list because this
    // game carries at most one of each, which makes `weapons[active_weapon]`
    // an index instead of a search.
    //
    // REPLICATED, because the client resolves its own ammo the same way the
    // server does -- `weapons[active_weapon]`, one index into a stored list --
    // rather than scanning every Weapon_Entity for one that claims this owner.
    // Deriving it client-side would be a second answer to "what am I carrying"
    // built out of a back-reference, and the two are free to disagree. It costs
    // one full update per player and then nothing: an inventory that does not
    // change is absent from every delta after the spawn.
    weapons:              u32[Weapon]     @Networked

    // Which of the above is in hand. The KEY, not a uid: resolution is
    // `weapons[active_weapon]`, one index, so there is no reference here that
    // can name a weapon the inventory does not hold.
    active_weapon:        Weapon = .Knife  @Networked

    // WHEN the switch in flight finishes, in sub-tick time; 0 means none. A
    // deadline like reload_complete_time above it, and for the same reason:
    // the duration belongs to the weapon being raised at the moment the key was
    // pressed, so settling it there beats re-deciding it every tick.
    //
    // This is the gate that is genuinely the PLAYER's -- it blocks every weapon
    // at once. A weapon's own fire interval is on the weapon, runs while
    // holstered, and the two must not be folded together: doing so is what made
    // firing a Scout delay a Knife swing.
    deploy_complete_time: u64
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Entities
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// @runtime_only marks a type the editor never places and the map never stores:
// it exists only inside a running session. It sits on the declaration, next to
// the `entity` keyword it modifies, for the same reason a field's flags sit
// next to the field -- it is a property of the type, not a member of it.
//
// Kept as a NEGATIVE (rather than defaulting to runtime-only and requiring
// @placeable) because "an entity is a thing you put in a level; some are
// spawned by code instead" is the mental model, and the majority case should
// be the silent one -- 5 of 8 today. It is also the safer default to forget:
// forgetting @runtime_only puts a junk type in the placement menu, which you
// see immediately; forgetting @placeable would make a real type quietly
// missing from it.
//
// It is load-bearing now, not decorative: placeable_entity_types() is
// generated by filtering on exactly this bit, and the two @Editable/@Saveable
// checks above key off it.
//
// Room to grow, if the need shows up -- same slot, same syntax:
//   @no_network   nothing about this type is ever replicated; makes @Networked
//                 on any of its fields an error. Trigger_Volume and
//                 Player_Spawn would both take it today.
//   @singleton    at most one per session (a gamemode / world-settings entity).
//   @category("Lighting")  grouping for the placement menu -- the first case
//                 that wants an ARGUMENT, which the grammar should be able to
//                 grow without moving the attribute somewhere else.

// Placed in the editor; the server consumes it at load and removes it.
// Nothing about a spawn point survives into a snapshot, so nothing is
// @Networked -- the client never sees one.
Player_Spawn_Entity :: entity {
    spawn_type: Spawn_Type = .Human     @Editable @Saveable
    team_allegiance: Team_Allegiance = .Free_For_All @Editable @Saveable 
}

// this does not need anything, really.
Player_Spectate_Entity:: entity {
}


// last_fire_weapon required because yhou need to know what you had equipped
// when firing in case weapon is switched
Player_Entity :: entity @runtime_only {
    // The remote player's model is oriented from these, so they replicate.
    view_angle_yaw:    f32              @Networked
    view_angle_pitch:  f32              @Networked
    body_yaw:          f32              @Networked

    health:            i32              @Networked

    death_tick:        u32              @Networked
    last_fire_tick:    u32              @Networked
    last_fire_weapon:  Weapon = .Knife  @Networked

    // WHEN the in-flight reload finishes, in sub-tick time; 0 means not
    // reloading. A DEADLINE rather than a start stamp, because the duration is
    // a property of the weapon being reloaded and the weapon can change while
    // the reload runs -- at a sub-tick moment inside the same step loop, now
    // that the weapon keys carry edges. Storing the start would leave "whose
    // duration applies" to be re-decided every tick against a weapon that is no
    // longer the one being reloaded; storing the deadline settles it at the
    // moment the input arrived, which is the only moment that knows.
    reload_complete_time: u64

    // Rate limit for the empty-magazine warning. Server-only, like the two
    // above: a log cadence is not something a client has any use for.
    last_empty_fire_warning_tick: u32
    last_hit_tick:         u32          @Networked
    last_hit_was_headshot: bool         @Networked


    // How a client works out which player entity is itself.
    client_slot_index: i32              @Networked
    // Feeds remote-player interpolation.
    velocity:          v3               @Networked

    inventory:         Inventory

    render:            Render = { mesh = .Leet_Full }
    team_allegiance: Team_Allegiance = .Free_For_All @Networked
}

// this is supposed to be a thing you pick up.
//
// REPLICATED as of the inventory work: entity_snapshot.cpp carries a map for
// these, so the two flags below are live rather than the inert pair TODO.md
// used to name. The client needs ammo -- it is the HUD's number and one of the
// gates play_predicted_local_gunshot re-runs -- and ammo belongs to the weapon,
// so the weapon is what has to arrive.
Weapon_Entity :: entity {
    // THE magazine. Was Player_Entity::ammo, one field for every weapon, which
    // is why switching had to hand out a fresh magazine to keep "ammo is the
    // held weapon's count" true -- a free instant reload on every keypress.
    ammo:             i32               @Networked
    weapon_id:        Weapon            @Networked

    // Whose inventory this is in; null_entity_uid means lying in the world.
    // The BACK-reference: Player_Entity::inventory.weapons is the authoritative
    // list, and this is what lets a weapon answer for itself -- teardown walks
    // it rather than trusting a player it may no longer belong to.
    //
    // NOT replicated, though the entity now is. The client resolves through the
    // forward list like the server does, so nothing over there reads this; a
    // @Networked here would be a second copy of the ownership edge on the wire
    // with no reader, which is exactly the inert flag TODO.md is about. A world
    // model on someone else's back is what would earn it.
    owner_uid:        u32

    // WHEN this weapon may next fire, in sub-tick time; 0 means immediately.
    // A deadline, not a last-fire stamp, so the interval is applied by the shot
    // that knows which weapon it came from.
    //
    // Per WEAPON, and it keeps running while holstered -- that is the whole
    // point. One clock per player meant the interval of whatever was in hand
    // was measured from whatever fired last, so firing a Scout delayed a Knife
    // swing and vice versa. The player-wide gate that DOES exist is
    // Inventory::deploy_complete_time, and the two are not the same thing.
    //
    // Server-only. The client re-runs this gate for its own predicted gunshot
    // audio, but off its own predicted clock -- a tick-granular copy on the
    // wire would be a second answer that could disagree with this one.
    next_fire_time:   u64

    render:           Render
}

Rocket_Entity :: entity @runtime_only {
    // Straight-line travel at 600 units/s: the client needs this the moment
    // snapshot interpolation lands, and has nothing else to extrapolate from.
    velocity:        v3                 @Networked
    // Server-only gameplay numbers. The client neither computes damage nor
    // draws a radius -- rocket_system.cpp is the only reader of all four, and
    // the server despawns on lifetime itself. Previously all @Networked, which
    // put 16 bytes of numbers on the wire per rocket for no reader.
    lifetime: f32 = 5.0
    damage_amount: f32 = 50.0
    damage_radius: f32 = 120.0
    knockback_force: f32 = 600.0
    owner_id:        u32
    // What rocket_system sweeps the flight path with. Was a whole Hitbox
    // component whose shape was always Sphere and whose offset was always zero;
    // the radius is the only part anything ever read. @Networked for the
    // debug_show_hitboxes overlay, which is the client's only interest in it.
    collision_radius: f32 = 12.0        @Networked
    render:          Render
}

// The client builds emitters from ITS OWN MAP LOAD, not from snapshots --
// play_state.cpp:1399 iterates map.entities_of_type<Particle_Emitter_Entity>().
// So nothing here is @Networked, and nothing should become @Networked one
// field at a time: the day an emitter is spawned at runtime (an explosion
// puff), the whole entity needs replicating and this block gets revisited as a
// block.
Particle_Emitter_Entity :: entity {
    sprite:             texture_asset = .Smoke @Editable @Saveable
    emit_rate:          f32 = 20.0            @Editable @Saveable
    max_particles:      i32 = 64              @Editable @Saveable
    lifetime_min:       f32 = 0.5             @Editable @Saveable
    lifetime_max:       f32 = 1.5             @Editable @Saveable
    velocity_min:       f32 = 2.0             @Editable @Saveable
    velocity_max:       f32 = 5.0             @Editable @Saveable
    spread:             f32 = 0.5             @Editable @Saveable
    gravity:            v3 = {0, 0.5, 0}      @Editable @Saveable
    drag:               f32 = 0.3             @Editable @Saveable
    size_start:         f32 = 0.5             @Editable @Saveable
    size_end:           f32 = 2.0             @Editable @Saveable
    rotation_speed_min: f32 = -1.0            @Editable @Saveable
    rotation_speed_max: f32 = 1.0             @Editable @Saveable
    color_start:        v3 = {1, 1, 1}        @Editable @Saveable
    color_end:          v3 = {0.5, 0.5, 0.5}  @Editable @Saveable
    alpha_start:        f32 = 0.8             @Editable @Saveable
    alpha_end:          f32 = 0.0             @Editable @Saveable

    // These two are DECLARED AND READ BY NOTHING -- grep finds no use outside
    // the schema registration itself. They were @Networked @Editable, which
    // claimed both a wire cost and an inspector row for a feature that does
    // not exist. Audited as:
    //   emitter_lifetime  a map author could plausibly want a one-shot emitter,
    //                     so it stays authorable and persisted.
    //   parent_entity_id  a uid means nothing in a map file (uids are assigned
    //                     per session), so it can only ever be runtime state
    //                     set by whoever spawns the emitter. No flags.
    emitter_lifetime:   f32 = 0.0             @Editable @Saveable
    parent_entity_id:   u32 = 0
}

// Invisible and entirely server-side: the server evaluates triggers, the
// client is never told one exists. Nothing here is @Networked, and Box_Volume
// above dropped @Networked for the same reason.
Trigger_Volume_Entity :: entity {
    // 64 is the editor's grid-sized starting box -- big enough to see and to
    // drag. Box_Volume's own {1,1,1} stays neutral for whatever embeds it next.
    volume:            Box_Volume = { half_extents = {64, 64, 64} }
    action:            Trigger_Action = .Kill   @Editable @Saveable
    fire_mode:         Fire_Mode = .On_Enter    @Editable @Saveable
    // 64 is enough for a spawn name to match against. param_string is
    // player-facing message text (Print_Message), so it gets room for a real
    // sentence -- 250 was never a chosen number, just the pascal_string_t
    // default every field inherited. Neither is networked, so the size costs
    // struct memory and map file bytes, not bandwidth. 255 is the hard ceiling.
    param_target_name: string<64>               @Editable @Saveable
    param_string:      string<128>              @Editable @Saveable
    param_float:       f32 = 0.0                @Editable @Saveable
}

// Lights are placed in the editor and read back out of the MAP -- and the
// client loads the same map, so it already has every one of them.
// @Networked on all seven fields was inherited from the macro declaration and
// replicated static config the client could not fail to have.
//
// Worth knowing while reading this: nothing in the renderer reads a
// Light_Entity yet. Its only consumers are the editor traits and picking
// bounds. When dynamic lights (muzzle flash, explosion) arrive they are
// runtime-spawned, and that is the change that earns @Networked back -- the
// same shape of argument as Render above.
Light_Entity :: entity {
    direction:          v3                     @Editable @Saveable
    color:              v3 = {1, 1, 1}         @Editable @Saveable
    intensity:          f32 = 1.0              @Editable @Saveable
    range:              f32                    @Editable @Saveable
    spot_inner_degrees: f32                    @Editable @Saveable
    spot_outer_degrees: f32                    @Editable @Saveable
    kind:               Light_Type = .Point    @Editable @Saveable
}

// Both map-placed AND runtime-spawned (the spawn_cube / spawn_sphere console
// commands), which is why its Render component has to be @Networked while its
// own configuration does not: play_state.cpp draws a body from
// body.position / body.orientation / body.render and reads nothing else off it.
Physics_Body_Entity :: entity {
    // Jolt's shape and the editor's size box. Server-side simulation input;
    // the visual size rides in render.scale, which the spawn path sets from
    // the same extents.
    shape:    Shape_Kind = .Box         @Editable @Saveable
    size:     v3                        @Editable @Saveable
    // Kept @Networked for the interpolation item in todo.md: bodies currently
    // snap to each snapshot, and velocity is what a lerp/extrapolate needs.
    velocity: v3                        @Networked
    mass:     f32 = 10.0                @Editable @Saveable
    render:   Render
}
